arty at all if you take a look at  internationally comparable opinion poll  surveys for instance the Japanese  people's trust in the government  institutions and the politicians they  elect is the lowest among OECD economies  I mean the countries with advanced  industrial democracies okay and  additionally violent oppressions of  opposition party leaders for instance  have been absent which had been problem  problems in many other what is called  electro authoritarian regimes Japan was  different from those types of political  systems okay so what happened what  happened to the this Democratic Liberal  Party it's yes I'm sorry it's no longer  in power so what happened there are  several factors I'm actually writing a  book first of all the government has  almost exhausted the monetary resources  out of which they could slice policy  benefits to their supporters secondary  recent decentralization reform heart the  party significantly because the LDP  effectively committed the political  organization of suicide by firing their  lawyer party activists are the local  community levels in Japan unlike many  other democracies in the world local  politicians  who serve in the municipal legislatures  and mayor's for the almost paid  activists for the Democratic Party and  because of the recent municipal  government consolidation efforts pursued  by the LDP a large number of politicians  at the local level lost jobs and because  they were no longer faced elektra  pressures for themselves they lost the  incentive to campaign for the party and  I am arguing that that was the largest  mistake the LDP government okay so what  lessons can we learn from the Japanese  experience  well democracy is more than periodic  elections in order to have a healthy  democratic governance in a country we  need something that cept democratic  institutions in particular electro  institutions are the labor contract  between the citizens and the political  leaders if those rules of elections are  po