ason was  voters were obviously frustrated with  the way politics  taking place in the area  second because everybody knew that my  predecessor would try to come back  sometimes nobody was seriously  considering running and I simply  volunteered to run but because Japanese  voters place more emphasis on our script  therefore descriptive representation  they want the representatives who look  similar to themselves for that  particular reason I didn't emphasize  very much of my education instead I the  fact that I grew up in the farm and my  parents are still farming okay alright  let's talk about your newest work I  understand you're you have a new book  you're working on can you tell us about  it  yes my project is about everything it's  going to be about the end of the LDP  dominance in Japan but I'm writing a  book so that it will be useful and  interesting for scholars who study other  political systems for instance politics  of China or politics of Latin America  it's an analytical monograph with solid  empirical evidence and I hope it will  clarify many of the important paradoxes  and puzzles that sustain the LDP's  dominance in Japan okay so but the LDP  is no longer dominating that's correct  right what is the new party the  Democratic Party of Japan which is the  party I was formally affiliated with  form the new government in Japan I like  the Liberal Democratic Party which is a  coalition of hereditary political  families and big businesses the DP jhf  primary support base includes  unorganized  consumers and labor union members okay  and are you working on anything else at  the moment in addition to the book there  are two new research projects that is on  work that are ongoing right now one of  them is about the tension between  diplomatic commitments and democratic  accountability for instance after the  DPJ took the helm of the japanese  government there has been a tension  between the US government and the  Japanese government regarding what to do  with the US military bases on the island  of Okinawa the DPJ is committed to their  supporters to reallocating relocating  military bases to somewhere else on the  other hand the LDP administration had  already cut a deal with the US  government and we expect a lot of  tensions between electoral  accountability and diplomatic  commitments Japanese government that  South Korean and Taiwanese government  faced a similar situation when they  experienced the partisan turnover in  their own government so I'm organizing  an international joint research project  that explored this important policy  issue aside from that this is my  personal aspiration but I'm thinking  about writing a book about political  culture and rice eating habit in East  Asian civilizations  okay can you expand on that a little bit  more I grew up on a farm as I have just  mentioned and the pattern of  collectivists oriented political  behavior is deeply rooted in rice for me  economic structures historically in East  Asian countries and I'm I'm interested  in exploring this more deeply  analytically okay very good well thank  you so much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your work  thank you very much it's my pleasure for  more information about Professor Saito  and his work please visit our website at  Yale edu backslash Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

